.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt CAP_PROCESS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cap_process
.Nd manage the calling process's capability discovery context and attribution
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/cap_process.h
.Ft int
.Fn cap_process "int op" "int fd" "uid_t uid" "void *data"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The discovery context is a kernel-held reference to a channel already owned
by the caller.
It survives descriptor cleanup and exec and is inherited by
children.
Replacing or clearing it affects only the calling process and future
children, not its parent, siblings, or existing children.
.Pp
The following operations are supported:
.Bl -tag -width MAC_CAP_PROCESS_ORIGIN_EXPORT
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_SET
Retain channel descriptor
.Fa fd
with the informational UID label
.Fa uid .
The label does not authenticate a user or grant authority.
The caller retains ownership of the descriptor.
Its Capsicum rights are copied.
Descriptors whose fork or exec propagation has been locked or restricted to
one use are rejected.
Installing a reference does not grant new authority.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_GET
Return an owned close-on-exec descriptor for the current discovery context.
Its rights are preserved and descriptor transfer is disabled.
The calling process's prison must match the installed context.
A UNIX UID change does not replace or invalidate the discovery route.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_CLEAR
Release the calling process's context.
Already returned descriptors and
contexts held by other processes remain valid.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_INFO
Copy a
.Vt struct mac_cap_process_info
into
.Fa data .
The structure reports this process's lifetime identity, responsible identity,
context generation, process IDs, informational UID label and whether a
context exists.
The same prison check as GET applies when a context exists.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_ORIGIN_EXPORT
Return an owned close-on-exec descriptor containing this process's immutable
lifetime identity and PID.
The token remains valid after the process exits.
.It Dv MAC_CAP_PROCESS_ORIGIN_SET
Associate the calling process with the origin token in
.Fa fd .
The token must originate in the same prison.
The association is inherited by
children and survives exec.
It grants no service authority and does not change
coalition membership or the caller's own identity.
.El
.Pp
.Fa data
must be NULL except for INFO.
Unused descriptor and UID arguments should be
-1 and zero respectively.
.Pp
A parent selects a different bootstrap for a child by forking, installing the
child's context in the child, then executing its program.
The discovery context supplies a route, not software attributes or service
authority.
Ordinary authentication and UNIX credential changes do not require a new
discovery context.
Service admission uses the requesting software's protected identity and
attributes; installing a route does not create or replace that identity.
The kernel-held reference does not reconnect to a terminated peer.
.Sh SOFTWARE AUTHORITY CONSTRAINT FLAGS
The same system call carries the software-authority operations declared in
.In sys/cap_authority.h .
They are an interface between the kernel and the system's issuer and are
not otherwise described here, except for the two points below that other
interfaces depend on.
.Pp
.Dv CAP_AUTH_CONSTRAIN
binds a managed authority to its executable images before it is installed.
Its request,
.Vt struct cap_authority_constraint ,
has a
.Va flags
word:
.Bl -tag -width CAP_AUTH_ROLE_DQ_SUPERVISOR
.It Dv CAP_AUTH_ROLE_DQ_SUPERVISOR
Mark the authority as a descriptor-queue supervisor.
The role requires exactly one image
.Pq Va nexec No of 1 .
An authority with a role cannot be registered as an application
.Pq Dv CAP_AUTH_REGISTER_APP No fails with Er EINVAL .
The role is written once with the constraint and cannot be added, removed
or changed afterwards; a child shares its parent's authority and there is
no operation that derives another from it.
.It Dv CAP_AUTH_ROLE_CORE
Mark the authority as a Core launch.
The same rules apply as for the supervisor role: exactly one image, written
once, never registered as an application.
The two roles may be combined.
When a process that is already excluded
.Pq see Sx BASE EXCLUSION
executes the image such an authority pins, with the authority installed,
the new image is excluded too.
The role grants nothing to a process that is not already excluded.
.El
.Pp
Every other bit, including the retired value 0x1, and a role with more than
one image, fails with
.Er EINVAL
before any descriptor in the request is examined.
A kernel that does not know a role refuses it the same way, which is how a
caller detects support.
A request whose
.Va flags
are zero behaves as it did before roles existed.
.Pp
The kernel's only requirement for setting a role is the one for the
constraint itself: the caller holds the issuer descriptor, with
.Dv CAP_WRITE ,
in the issuer's prison.
The kernel does not examine a bundle, a signature or an entitlement.
The policy that a role is given only to a program from a signed bundle that
carries the entitlement for it belongs to the issuer,
.Xr switchboard 8 ;
this bit only carries the issuer's decision.
At present no kernel interface consults the supervisor role;
.Sx BASE EXCLUSION
consults the Core role.
.Sh SOFTWARE AUTHORITY OF AN ATTACHED PROCESS
A process attached to a descriptor queue
.Po Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH ,
.Xr dq 4
.Pc
loses the use of its software authority at the attach, whether the
authority was valid or installed and still waiting for its image.
.Dv CAP_AUTH_INFO
then reports the same identity, a
.Va valid
of zero and a
.Va generation
advanced by one, as it does after the process moves to another prison;
operations on endpoints bound to the authority fail with
.Er EPERM ,
and the process's invocation binding is dropped.
The state is inherited by children and is not undone by
.Xr execve 2 :
.Dv CAP_AUTH_INSTALL
is refused for an attached process and no image activates an authority in
one.
.Dv CAP_AUTH_CLEAR
still removes the authority.
A process whose authority was already invalid, or that has none, is not
changed, and neither is the attaching process.
.Pp
Until the attached process executes an image, the stripped authority
continues to decide who may debug it: a process that is not itself stripped
gets the answer it got before the attach, so one that could not debug it
because of the authority still cannot.
Once it has executed an image the authority no longer restricts debugging
it.
.Pp
The stripped process itself no longer passes that check as a debugger.
It cannot debug the holder of the authority, another process stripped of
the same authority, or a child it forks, and
.Dv PT_TRACE_ME
in such a child fails; the error is
.Er EPERM .
Before the attach all of these were allowed.
The refusal towards a stripped process or a child ends when that process
executes an image, because the authority then stops restricting who may
debug it.
The refusal towards the holder does not end: the holder is protected for as
long as it holds the authority, also after it executes an image, and the
stripped process never acts with the authority again.
An image executed by the stripped process itself changes only who may debug
that process.
Nothing else is taken at the attach: the discovery context described above
and endpoints that are not bound to an authority are unchanged.
.Sh BASE EXCLUSION
The kernel marks some processes as excluded from observation and
interposition by
.Xr oes 4
and by Capability Brokers
.Pq Xr dq 4 :
process 1, and any image a process that is already excluded executes
under a valid installed authority that carries
.Dv CAP_AUTH_ROLE_CORE
and pins that image.
Exclusion is decided at every
.Xr execve 2 ,
before the image is checked: any other image clears it.
Exclusion is handed on, never acquired: a process that is not excluded
cannot become excluded, whatever authority it installs.
An exec that would be excluded fails with
.Er EACCES
if the image is a script or binmisc image or uses a non-native ABI, except
in process 1.
Exclusion is inherited by
.Xr fork 2
and cleared when the process is traced with
.Xr ptrace 2
or
.Xr ktrace 2 ,
or enters another jail.
Exclusion grants no privilege.
.Xr ps 1
shows it as
.Dv P2_CAP_EXCLUDED
.Pq 0x20000000
in
.Cm flags2 .
The
.Va security.cap_exclusion
sysctl tree counts exclusions granted
.Pq Va granted
and excluded executions refused
.Pq Va refused_interp , refused_abi ;
the
.Nm cap_exclusion
DTrace provider fires
.Fn exec pid excluded
at every exec commit,
.Fn clear pid reason
when exclusion is cleared otherwise, and
.Fn refuse pid reason errno
on every refusal.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
GET and ORIGIN_EXPORT return a descriptor on success.
Other operations return
zero.
On failure, -1 is returned and errno is set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENOTCAPABLE
.It Bq Er ENOENT
GET was requested without an installed context.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The discovery context or origin token belongs to another prison.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
Installing the descriptor would bypass its fork or exec restrictions, or an
operation exceeds its Capsicum rights.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The operation, object type, or data argument is invalid.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The supplied descriptor is invalid.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The calling process cannot allocate another descriptor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The INFO output address is invalid.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr closefrom 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr dq 4
